   "grade": "Likely (within about 1°) / Suspected as dating",
   "verdict": "In 2560 BC the four stars crossed the meridian -0.33°, -1.14°, -0.62° and 0.62° from the shaft angles. Each shaft matches its star exactly in a different year: KS 2492 BC, KN 2357 BC, QS 2359 BC, QN 2383 BC. The spread is 135 years, and all four dates fall after Khufu's reign, so the shafts cannot date the pyramid to better than about a century (Thuban also fits a second time, in 3237 BC, on its way in to the pole); and the shafts bend on their way out, so 'the' angle is itself a choice (Petrie and Gantenbrink differ by up to 2°).",
